    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The lines were made between 500 BCE and 500 CE, in a Paracas phase (400 to 200 BCE) and a Nazca phase (200 BCE to 500 CE), by removing the reddish-brown pebbles of the desert surface to show the lighter subsoil.</li><li>Scholars differ over their purpose but in general ascribe religious significance to them; astronomical, water-related, ceremonial and calendrical explanations have each been argued, while the idea of ancient astronauts has been refuted.</li><li>Drones and machine learning have raised the number of known figures from a few dozen to hundreds in the 21st century, and archaeologists expect more to be found.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Joe Nickell reproduced the figures in the early 21st century with tools of the kind the Nazca people had, and found that a small team could recreate even the largest figures within days, without aerial assistance.</p></aside>
    </section>

